<Project>
  <!-- ===================================================================
       Edition isolation (design doc §7.3 / §18.1).

       Perpetual is the buy-out product and promises, in public, that it does
       not go online. That promise is only worth something if it is structural
       rather than a switch somebody can flip: if the networking code sits in
       the buy-out binary behind a disabled branch, "does not go online" is
       just a flag. So the subscription-only sources are not compiled into
       Perpetual at all. There is no IL, no metadata, and no string to find
       later.
       =================================================================== -->

  <!-- Remove, not a conditional Include.
       SDK-style projects glob their sources into @(Compile) from the implicit
       SDK imports. Directory.Build.targets is imported after the project body
       but before Sdk.targets, which is the one place where Remove can still
       act on those defaults: the same expressions in .props are evaluated
       before the glob exists and would silently match nothing. -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(WeChatBridgeEdition)' != 'Subscription'">
    <Compile Remove="Cloud\**\*.cs" />
    <Compile Remove="CloudQr\**\*.cs" />
    <Compile Remove="Billing\**\*.cs" />
    <Page Remove="CloudQr\**\*.xaml" />
    <Page Remove="Billing\**\*.xaml" />
    <Resource Remove="CloudQr\**\*" />
    <Resource Remove="Billing\**\*" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Redeem/ and Licensing/ are deliberately absent from that list. The
       redeem client is the one thing the buy-out product is allowed to reach
       the network for (§7.2 option B), and it lives in Redeem/. Its one
       request is the exception the published privacy wording names; excluding
       the directory would make the wording false. -->

  <!-- ===================================================================
       Make the edition an input to CoreCompile.

       WeChatBridgeEdition is a property, and MSBuild decides CoreCompile is
       up to date from timestamps of files, not from properties. So building
       Subscription straight after Perpetual, with no source edited in between,
       leaves the previous edition's assembly sitting in bin\ wearing the new
       edition's name in the build log. Verified here: after switching to
       Subscription, ShareTarget.dll still carried 1.0.8 while Core.dll had
       been rebuilt as 0.1.0, so one output folder held both editions.

       That is worse than a stale-file nuisance. The version split is the only
       thing that tells support which product a customer is running, and the
       Compile Remove above is the only thing keeping network code out of the
       buy-out binary. Both of those are silently wrong when the compiler is
       allowed to skip work it should have done.

       @(CustomAdditionalCompileInputs) is the SDK's own extension point for
       this: it is listed in CoreCompile's Inputs, so putting the stamp file
       there re-runs CoreCompile when the stamp is newer than the output. Not
       $(CoreCompileTimeInputs), which reads like the right answer and is
       dead: the property appears nowhere in the .NET 10 SDK, so setting it
       changes nothing at all and the guard reads as working while it does not.

       WriteOnlyWhenDifferent is what keeps this incremental rather than
       brutal: the stamp holds the edition name and is rewritten only when that
       name changes, so a rebuild of the same edition stays a no-op and only a
       switch of edition costs a compile.
       =================================================================== -->
  <Target Name="WcbEditionStampIsACompileInput" BeforeTargets="CoreCompile">
    <PropertyGroup>
      <_WcbEditionStampFile>$(IntermediateOutputPath)WeChatBridgeEdition.txt</_WcbEditionStampFile>
    </PropertyGroup>
    <ItemGroup>
      <CustomAdditionalCompileInputs Include="$(_WcbEditionStampFile)" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <WriteLinesToFile File="$(_WcbEditionStampFile)"
                      Lines="$(WeChatBridgeEdition)"
                      Overwrite="true"
                      WriteOnlyWhenDifferent="true" />
  </Target>

  <!-- ===================================================================
       Guard against an Include reaching past the Remove above.

       The Remove cannot see a file that comes back in through an explicit
       or linked <Compile Include>: someone pulling a subscription source in
       from outside the project is the failure mode the glob will not report,
       and it would otherwise surface only as a network call in the field.

       This reads @(Compile) and tests each item's own path, rather than
       globbing the excluded directories off disk and subtracting @(Compile).
       Globbing cannot see an Include spelled ..\..\shared-src\Cloud\Foo.cs:
       MSBuild does not expand ** across a `..` segment, and a linked source
       is precisely the case this target exists for. Both of the earlier
       formulation's defects were confirmed against this tree before it was
       replaced — the ** glob returned nothing for a linked file, and the
       Exclude that was supposed to subtract @(Compile) subtracted nothing,
       so a single on-disk Cloud/ file made the target report every source in
       the project. A check that cries wolf the moment the feature it guards
       starts being written is not a check.

       A path segment, not a substring: the separators on both sides keep
       WeChatBridge.Windows.Core.Cloud\Foo.cs and Cloudy\Foo.cs out. Matched
       case-insensitively, because the Remove globs above are, and Windows
       paths are not case-sensitive — a directory spelled billing must not slip
       past the exclusion it was given.

       Rooted identities are skipped: an absolute Include reaches the same
       directory by a name this test has no business interpreting.

       Residual false positive, accepted deliberately: a checkout living under
       a directory literally named Cloud, CloudQr or Billing fails the build
       with WCB0002. It is loud and it names the offending items, so the fix
       is obvious, and failing closed is the right way round for a check whose
       entire value is that nothing ships unexamined.

       Written with string Contains rather than a regex because MSBuild's
       condition parser does not survive the character class: `[\\/]` comes
       back from Regex::IsMatch with the escape mangled and matches nothing,
       silently turning the whole guard off. Verified before relying on it.
       =================================================================== -->
  <Target Name="WcbAssertNoSubscriptionSources"
          BeforeTargets="CoreCompile"
          Condition="'$(WeChatBridgeEdition)' != 'Subscription'">
    <ItemGroup>
      <_WcbLeakedSource
          Include="@(Compile)"
          Condition="!$([System.IO.Path]::IsPathRooted('%(Compile.Identity)')) and ($([System.String]::Copy('\%(Compile.Identity)').Replace('/','\').ToUpperInvariant().Contains('\CLOUD\')) or $([System.String]::Copy('\%(Compile.Identity)').Replace('/','\').ToUpperInvariant().Contains('\CLOUDQR\')) or $([System.String]::Copy('\%(Compile.Identity)').Replace('/','\').ToUpperInvariant().Contains('\BILLING\')))" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <Error Code="WCB0002"
           Condition="'@(_WcbLeakedSource)' != ''"
           Text="Perpetual must not compile subscription-only sources, but these reached the compile item list by a route the Compile Remove above cannot see (an explicit or linked Compile Include, most likely): @(_WcbLeakedSource). Drop the Include, or move the file out of Cloud/, CloudQr/ or Billing/." />
  </Target>
</Project>
